# Logitech G502 X PLUS (046d:4099) -> uaccess on its own hidraw node, and its
# event node to the gearbench group to read.
#
# Matched by the mouse's HID device name rather than by udev properties.
# The mouse is paired through a receiver, and its hidraw node inherits the
# receiver's properties verbatim: /dev/hidraw9 and the receiver's own
# interface 2 both report ID_MODEL_ID=c547 and ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM=02, so a
# property match cannot tell them apart and would grant access to the
# receiver's raw interfaces as well.
#
# That distinction is the point. A Lightspeed receiver can have a keyboard
# paired to it too, and its interfaces carry the input reports of everything
# paired. The device node below carries only this mouse's reports, its motion
# and buttons as well as its HID++ traffic, but for the exception below.
#
# Pair nothing else to this mouse's receiver. Read from the kernel's
# hid-logitech-dj.c on 2026-09-27 and not checked on hardware: the driver
# marks every device on a Lightspeed link
# as taking keyboard reports, and hands each keyboard report to the first
# such device in slot order. With a keyboard paired after the mouse,
# its keystrokes would arrive on this mouse's hidraw and event nodes, the
# two this file grants. In host mode, when a key arrives on the event node,
# the daemon warns in the journal once each time the mouse attaches, and
# `gearbench-ctl status`, the tray and the Mouse tab show the warning until
# the mouse detaches.
#
# KERNELS matches a parent's kernel name, which for the paired device is
# bus:vendor:product.instance -> 0003:046D:4099.<n>.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="0003:046D:4099.*", TAG+="uaccess"

# The mouse's own evdev node, so the daemon can read the extra buttons.
#
# From the factory G6, G7, G8 and G9 (the paddle, DPI down, DPI up and the
# button behind the wheel) emit nothing at all: they are bound to firmware
# functions. `gearbench-ctl mouse-enable-buttons` rewrites the mouse's onboard
# profile 1 (0x8100) so they and the two side buttons G4 and G5 send mouse
# buttons 11-16, which the kernel reports on this node as key codes
# 0x11a-0x11f. Nothing on the desktop acts on those, so the daemon reads them
# here and acts on them itself. In host mode, the default, G4 to G9 come
# through the 0x8110 spy on the hidraw node above once it is armed.
#
# Deliberately not solved by adding the user to the "input" group: that grants
# read access to every input device on the system, the keyboard included, and
# a process that can read the keyboard's event node is a keylogger. This rule
# grants one node, belonging to this mouse.
#
# It cannot be scoped as tightly as the cabled rule below. Cabled, this mouse
# presents two event nodes and only one of them is the pointer, so interface
# 00 is granted and the keyboard collection on 01 is not. Through the receiver
# there is one node for the whole mouse: measured 2026-09-22, it declares 191
# keyboard-range codes alongside the 0x11a-0x11f the extra buttons arrive on.
# Reading those buttons means granting that node, and no finer scope exists.
#
# What this does and does not expose: the node carries what this mouse emits,
# its motion and buttons, with G4 to G9 as buttons 11-16 after
# mouse-enable-buttons. It does not carry the G915's keystrokes, which come on
# the G915's own interfaces that no rule here grants; a keyboard paired to
# this mouse's receiver is the exception above. ID_INPUT_MOUSE bounds it to
# pointer-capable nodes, so a second node the mouse might expose is not swept
# in with it.
#
# The grant is the gearbench group's, mode 0640 (the owner's decision of
# 2026-09-28). uaccess would give the seat user a read-write ACL, and an
# input_event written to an event node reaches every reader of that device,
# the compositor included, so a program that could open this node, a Flatpak
# given --device=input among them, could type through its 191 keyboard-range
# codes. The daemon only reads it: gearbench/evdev.py opens it O_RDONLY and
# never grabs it.
#
# A member of the group reads the node from any session, the seat's or not,
# and none can write events to it. The evdev ioctls do not check the open
# mode, though: a member's program holding it open, read-only, can still grab
# it (EVIOCGRAB, which takes the mouse from the session until it lets go) or
# remap its buttons (EVIOCSKEYCODE) until a replug (read from the kernel's
# source on 2026-09-28, not checked on hardware). The
# input group, which udev's default rules give
# every event node, loses it; the compositor opens it through logind and
# keeps it. udev looks the group up when it reads this file and drops the
# whole line when there is no such group, so install.sh and the package
# make it first.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNELS=="0003:046D:4099.*", KERNEL=="event*", ENV{ID_INPUT_MOUSE}=="1", GROUP="gearbench", MODE="0640"

# The same mouse, cabled (046d:c095).
#
# Read off the hardware on 2026-09-22: cabled it has a USB identity of its
# own and three interfaces, exactly like the keyboard. 00 is the pointer
# (usage page 0x01, usage 0x02), 01 is a keyboard collection (usage 0x06),
# and 02 is the vendor HID++ collection (usage page 0xFF00). So the property
# match that cannot work through the receiver works here, and it is scoped
# the same way the keyboard's is.
#
# Interface 01 is deliberately absent. This mouse has a keyboard collection
# of its own, so the reasoning that keeps the daemon off the keyboard's
# interfaces 00 and 01 applies to the mouse just as much.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c095", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="02", TAG+="uaccess"

# The pointer's event node, for the extra buttons -- interface 00 only, for
# the reason above. Mode 0640 to the gearbench group, as through the
# receiver: a program that could write this node could move the pointer and
# click. The ioctls above apply here too.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c095", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="00", GROUP="gearbench", MODE="0640"
